  "semantic_checklist": [
    {
      "id": "C01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Attached printed page 1 contains (1.2), (1.3), and unchanged-shape propagation. Printed pages 4–5 and 215–216 supply integral and weak-solution context.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation concerns a scalar translated profile, its characteristic identities, and conditional differential and integral solution properties.",
      "reasoning": "The selected result and relevant inherited context are correctly located. Source identity uses the supplied hash; no independent byte-hash verification was performed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 1 introduces real space and time coordinates, specializes to m=1, and takes a constant scalar velocity.",
      "translation_evidence": "Profile, coordinates, time, speed, flux values, and derivative witnesses are real; the generic vector-space dependencies specialize to real values.",
      "reasoning": "Scalar types, dimension, and coordinate domains agree."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.3) specifies a solution for each profile and fixed velocity, with no new restriction on x or t.",
      "translation_evidence": "Profile and c are universally quantified, q is defined from them, and pointwise derivative witnesses occur inside the quantification over x,t.",
      "reasoning": "Parameter dependence and pointwise witness scope are preserved. The two regularity conditions belong to separate implications."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 1 gives arbitrary-profile wording without an admissibility class. Pages 4–5 distinguish smooth classical solutions from discontinuous integral solutions.",
      "translation_evidence": "The differential claim assumes everywhere differentiability; the integral claim assumes interval integrability on every finite interval with respect to an unidentified fixed measure μ.",
      "reasoning": "Differentiability appropriately guards actual partial derivatives. Local interval integrability is an explicit interpretive addition whose correspondence to the source's unstated admissible class cannot be established conclusively."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The selected passage asserts both a solution family and constant-speed propagation with unchanged shape.",
      "translation_evidence": "The formula and characteristic identity hold unconditionally. Differential and integral solution properties are conditional.",
      "reasoning": "The geometric conclusion is complete. Whether the conditional analytic claims cover the source's intended solution-family claim depends on admissibility and the unresolved integration measure."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) uses ordinary spatial integration and physical flux; the contextual weak formulation likewise integrates in real space and time.",
      "translation_evidence": "D001–D004 explain the solution predicates; D024 and D081 explain interval integrability and oriented integration. D055 expressly leaves the selected real measure unidentified.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied ledger explains the derivative, algebraic, and logical operations, but does not establish that μ gives the source's ordinary integration semantics. Declaration inspection is excluded in this role."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equations (1.2) and (1.3) are exact equalities. Equation (1.10) uses incoming flux minus outgoing flux.",
      "translation_evidence": "All identities are exact, the PDE is qt+c·qx=0, and integral change equals the time integral of c·q(a,τ)−c·q(b,τ).",
      "reasoning": "Signs, equality strength, and flux orientation agree. The semantic sufficiency of the conditional formulation is separately unresolved."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The profile q̃, evolving field q, and constant velocity ū have distinct roles.",
      "translation_evidence": "Profile defines q(x,t)=profile(x−c·t), while F(z)=c·z is a separate flux function.",
      "reasoning": "Profile, solution, velocity, and flux are not conflated. The fixed characteristic label is distinguished from the evolving spatial coordinate."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The same ū occurs in q_t+ūq_x=0, x−ūt, and the advection flux ūq.",
      "translation_evidence": "The same constant c occurs in the translated argument, PDE, characteristic path, and flux.",
      "reasoning": "Coefficients, signs, parameter dependence, and characteristic direction match."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The selected assertion has real scalar values and unrestricted real coordinates, but no precise profile space.",
      "translation_evidence": "The same coordinate and value domains are retained; analytic solution properties are restricted by differentiability or interval integrability.",
      "reasoning": "No dimensional or temporal specialization occurs. Whether the analytic restrictions merely make implicit admissibility precise or omit intended cases remains unresolved."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source imposes no sign restriction on velocity and explicitly denies classical PDE validity at discontinuities.",
      "translation_evidence": "Positive, negative, and zero speeds are included. Actual partial derivatives require differentiability; the unrestricted derivative is only of a constant characteristic composition. Oriented integrals include reversed and equal endpoints.",
      "reasoning": "The translation does not assert partial derivatives at jumps. Stationary transport and endpoint degeneracies are handled consistently."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The conservation statement expresses physical mass balance over spatial intervals.",
      "translation_evidence": "The geometric and smooth differential claims are nonvacuous. The conservation clause uses μ, whose identity is unresolved in D055.",
      "reasoning": "There is no apparent impossible global premise, but the ledger does not exclude a measure that trivializes conservation or changes its admissible profiles. Nonvacuity of the intended integral semantics therefore remains unverified."
    },
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The selected process is exact constant-speed transport of an unchanged profile.",
      "translation_evidence": "q(x,t)=profile(x−c·t) and q(x+c·t,t)=profile(x) explicitly encode that process.",
      "reasoning": "The proposition remains linked to the specified transport. No numerical algorithm is part of the selected result."
    },
    {
      "id": "N02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The selected setting uses ordinary real scalar values and classical real differentiation.",
      "translation_evidence": "The ledger identifies the usual real norm and distance |x−y|, with real-valued derivatives.",
      "reasoning": "The analytic topology and scalar interpretation agree; no alternative error norm or operator norm is introduced. The integration measure is separately unresolved."
    },
    {
      "id": "N03",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected passage asserts an exact transport solution, without an error or stability estimate.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation contains exact identities and solution predicates, without approximation errors or stability bounds.",
      "reasoning": "There is no error or stability notion to compare."
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.3) describes an exact evolving field.",
      "translation_evidence": "The constructed q is exactly profile(x−c·t); no computed or rounded quantity occurs.",
      "reasoning": "Exact quantities retain their source roles."
    },
    {
      "id": "N05",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected result contains no asymptotic expansion, truncation, or limiting regime.",
      "translation_evidence": "No asymptotic terms, remainders, or approximation limits appear.",
      "reasoning": "There is no asymptotic claim to compare."
    },
    {
      "id": "N06",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The equation admits the displayed family; uniqueness is not selected. Classical and nonclassical solution interpretations are distinguished in context.",
      "translation_evidence": "An explicit family is constructed with conditional classical and integral properties, without uniqueness or exhaustiveness.",
      "reasoning": "No unsupported uniqueness claim is added. Coverage of the intended existence claim remains dependent on the unspecified source admissibility class and unidentified measure."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "The translation implies the exact profile formula, initial identity, unchanged-shape propagation, and classical PDE validity for differentiable profiles. Its integral clause could cover the intended discontinuous solutions if μ has the source's integration semantics and its interval-integrable profiles supply the intended admissible class. Neither identification is established by the permitted evidence. The unconditional characteristic identity alone does not establish integral or weak solutionhood for every arbitrary profile."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "The formula implies the initial and characteristic identities and the zero derivative of the constant characteristic composition; ordinary differentiation also yields the conditional classical PDE. The full final conjunct involves a fixed but unidentified measure, slice integrability, and all-time integrated balance under a precise interval-integrability assumption absent from the selected wording. The source cannot determine that measure-specific assertion from the supplied evidence."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "requires_adjudication": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-integration-measure",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 4, (1.10), interprets spatial integration as total mass and uses the advection flux ūq.",
      "translation_evidence": "D055 and the translation's ambiguity explicitly state that the identity and normalization of μ are not exposed.",
      "impact": "The conservation predicate's effective domain and correspondence to physical interval integration cannot be verified. Adjudication must establish the selected measure's semantics; this is an evidence gap, not a demonstrated incorrect measure."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-profile-admissibility",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 1 supplies arbitrary-profile wording; pages 4–5 and 215 distinguish classical from integral or weak solutions without specifying a complete admissible profile class for (1.3).",
      "translation_evidence": "Only transport geometry is unconditional. Classical solutionhood requires differentiability, and integral solutionhood requires interval integrability on every finite interval.",
      "impact": "The translation plausibly supplies a precise interpretation, but equivalence with the selected broad solution assertion cannot be certified without resolving the source's intended admissibility. These hypotheses must not be counted automatically as genuine strengthening."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "characteristic-derivative",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.3) preserves the profile along x=ξ+ūt, while page 5 excludes classical PDE validity at jumps.",
      "translation_evidence": "The unrestricted derivative concerns τ↦q(x+c·τ,τ); partial derivative existence is separately conditional.",
      "impact": "The unrestricted zero derivative is a valid consequence of a constant composition and does not incorrectly differentiate a discontinuous profile."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The attached primary-source images support the translation's transport geometry, scalar domains, coefficient signs, flux orientation, and distinction between characteristic constancy and classical partial derivatives. No definite mathematical mismatch is established. Acceptance nevertheless requires resolving the measure left unidentified by the blind ledger and the relationship between the source's unspecified admissibility and the translation's conditional analytic claims. Both implication directions therefore remain unclear, requiring adjudication."
